{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And Abraham breathed his last and died in a good old age, an old man and full.</b> The Ramban explained, and likewise Rabbi David Kimchi, that he was sated from all the desires of this world — unlike the wicked, who do not depart from the world with even half their desire in hand, but he was sated from everything. But concerning Isaac, of whom it is written (Genesis 35:29) ״זָקֵן וּשְׂבַע יָמִים,״ the expression does not carry that meaning, for the word ״שָׂבֵעַ״ is construed upon the days — that he was sated in days, not sated from the desires of this world. Here too, why did he hang the matter upon his old age, for even in his earlier days he lacked nothing? And further, why did he mention first the hoary age (שֵׂיבָה) and afterward old age (זִקְנָה) — are not the days of old age prior to the days of hoary age?" "Therefore it appears more correct to say that even in the case of Abraham, ״וְשָׂבֵעַ״ refers to the days; and even though ״יָמִים״ is not mentioned here, nevertheless from the fact that he mentioned the שֵׂיבָה, the זִקְנָה is also implied as referring to the days, as will be explained shortly. But in the case of Isaac, who did not mention the שֵׂיבָה, it was necessary to state ״יָמִים״ explicitly — for since the primary reward of the righteous is for the world to come, therefore they are eager and reject the life of this world, and all their hope and desire is to bask in the light of eternal life and to enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah, for in this world ״לֹא יִרְאַנִי הָאָדָם וָחָי״ (Exodus 33:20). Therefore the wicked, who are compared to darkness regarding the world to come, and whose tranquility is primarily in this world — they are never sated in days, for they know that they have no portion there, and all their desire is for the days of this world. But the righteous are sated in the days and have no desire for them, as David said (Psalms 39:5): ״הוֹדִיעֵנִי ה׳ קִצִּי וּמִדַּת יָמַי מַה הִיא, אֵדְעָה מֶה חָדֵל אָנִי.״ That is to say: that I may know how much I am lacking from the true good of the world to come. Therefore it is said of the righteous that they are ״שְׂבֵעֵי יָמִים,״ for every satiety is a thing in which a person has no further desire. Therefore he put ״יְמֵי הַשֵׂיבָה״ first, to tell you: not only in the days of his hoary age was he sated in days, but even in the days of his old age he was sated from them, for so great was his desire to enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah, and he rejected the life of this world all his days, for he yearned intensely for his Father's house in heaven." "<b>And was gathered to his people.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Bava Batra 16a) that the terms גְּוִיעָה and אֲסִיפָה are used only of the righteous. And the meaning of this אֲסִיפָה appears to me to be as follows, based on what our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said (Rosh HaShanah 17a): that the wicked are judged in Gehinnom for twelve months — their body is consumed and their soul is burned and the wind scatters them and they become ash, etc. And אֲסִיפָה means the opposite of scattering — that the wind does not scatter them. And we find the expression אֲסִיפָה used for gathering into a house, as in ״וַאֲסַפְתּוֹ אֶל תּוֹךְ בֵּיתֶךָ״ (Deuteronomy 22:2), ״וְאֵין אִיש מְאַסֵּף אוֹתָם הַבָּיְתָה״ (Judges 19:15). And this means that the glory of HaShem gathers them to Himself, blessed be He — to exclude the wicked, who are banished from the presence of the Shekhinah. And as for the fact that אֲסִיפָה is stated concerning Ishmael — it is because he repented, as it is written ״וַיִּקְבְּרוּ אֹתוֹ יִצְחָק וְיִשְׁמָעֵאל״ (Genesis 25:9), that he let Isaac walk before him. And if it is a tradition from our Rabbis, of blessed memory, we accept it; and if it is according to reasoning, there is an answer, for one may explain the verse from the other side: that he was so wicked that he had no regard for his father's honor and did not involve himself in his burial, until Isaac began first to attend to the burial, and therefore out of shame he too involved himself in the burial. Nevertheless, one should not depart from the tradition of our Rabbis, of blessed memory, and a proof for their words is that surely all the sons of the concubines he sent away from Isaac while he was still alive, and he surely did not send Ishmael away — for if not, how would he have been there at the time of his burial? Rather, he surely repented and joined himself to Isaac and let him walk before him."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {2 "The commentary cites Rabbi David Kimchi (רבינו בחיי in the text appears to be a scribal substitution for רד\"ק, since the reading aligns with Kimchi's interpretation, not Rabbenu Behaye's); translated the name as it stands in the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:30:09.467005230Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}